  {
    "question": "What are the two main difficulties in knowledge representation for AI?",
    "answer": "The two main difficulties are the enormous breadth of commonsense knowledge (the vast set of atomic facts an average person knows) and the sub-symbolic form of most commonsense knowledge (much of what people know is not represented as verbalizable facts or statements).",
    "estimated_difficulty": 6,
    "citations": ["Among the most difficult problems in knowledge representation are the breadth of commonsense knowledge (the set of atomic facts that the average person knows is enormous); and the sub-symbolic form of most commonsense knowledge (much of what people know is not represented as \"facts\" or \"statements\" that they could express verbally)."]
  },
  {
    "question": "How did reasoning models, a type of large language model introduced in 2024, improve AI performance?",
    "answer": "Reasoning models, trained to generate intermediate chains-of-thought, allowed improved performance on complex problems in mathematics and coding.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 4,
    "citations": ["Reasoning models, a type of large language model trained to generate intermediate chains-of-thought, emerged in 2024 and allowed improved performance on complex problems in mathematics and coding."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the key difference between automated planning and automated decision-making for an agent?",
    "answer": "In automated planning, the agent has a specific goal. In automated decision-making, the agent has preferences—some situations it prefers and some it tries to avoid, and it assigns a utility to measure these preferences.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 5,
    "citations": ["In automated planning, the agent has a specific goal. In automated decision-making, the agent has preferences—there are some situations it would prefer to be in, and some situations it is trying to avoid. The decision-making agent assigns a number to each situation (called its \"utility\") that measures how much the agent prefers it."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a Markov decision process composed of, and what does a policy define?",
    "answer": "A Markov decision process has a transition model that describes the probability an action changes the state and a reward function that supplies the utility of each state and cost of each action. A policy associates a decision with each possible state.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 8,
    "citations": ["A Markov decision process has a transition model that describes the probability that a particular action will change the state in a particular way and a reward function that supplies the utility of each state and the cost of each action. A policy associates a decision with each possible state."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a rational agent, as defined in the context of AI planning and decision-making?",
    "answer": "A rational agent is any entity (artificial or not) that perceives and takes actions in the world, has goals or preferences, and takes actions to make them happen.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 3,
    "citations": ["A rational agent has goals or preferences and takes actions to make them happen.[d]"]
  },
  {
    "question": "Why were early AI algorithms for reasoning and problem-solving insufficient for large problems?",
    "answer": "They were insufficient because they experienced a \"combinatorial explosion,\" becoming exponentially slower as the problems grew.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 4,
    "citations": ["Many of these algorithms are insufficient for solving large reasoning problems because they experience a \"combinatorial explosion\": They become exponentially slower as the problems grow."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What additional challenge in knowledge representation is mentioned alongside the breadth and form of commonsense knowledge?",
    "answer": "The difficulty of knowledge acquisition, which is the problem of obtaining knowledge for AI applications.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 5,
    "citations": ["There is also the difficulty of knowledge acquisition, the problem of obtaining knowledge for AI applications.[c]."]
  },
  {
    "question": "In classical planning versus most real-world problems, what are the key uncertainties an agent faces?",
    "answer": "In classical planning, the agent knows exactly what the effect of any action will be. In most real-world problems, the agent may be uncertain about the situation it is in (it is 'unknown' or 'unobservable') and may not know for certain what will happen after each possible action (it is not 'deterministic').",
    "estimated_difficulty": 7,
    "citations": ["In classical planning, the agent knows exactly what the effect of any action will be. In most real-world problems, however, the agent may not be certain about the situation it is in (it is \"unknown\" or \"unobservable\") and it may not know for certain what will happen after each possible action (it is not \"deterministic\")."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the purpose of an ontology in the context of knowledge representation?",
    "answer": "An ontology is the set of objects, relations, concepts, and properties used by a particular domain of knowledge.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 5,
    "citations": ["An ontology is the set of objects, relations, concepts, and properties used by a particular domain of knowledge."]
  },
  {
    "question": "How is game theory used in AI programs?",
    "answer": "Game theory describes the rational behavior of multiple interacting agents and is used in AI programs that make decisions that involve other agents.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 6,
    "citations": ["Game theory describes the rational behavior of multiple interacting agents and is used in AI programs that make decisions that involve other agents."]
  }
]

